                    coach_r1_nodiluter:     `<strong>“Nothing is dilutive” is the comfortable answer.</strong> Profitable in absolute terms is not the same as efficient per faculty-day. Some line is earning far less per scarce day than another — find it, because that's where capacity is leaking.`,
                    coach_r1_strength:      `<strong>The anchor is the crown jewel — and a single point of failure.</strong> One client at 28% of revenue is strategic concentration risk, and they're about to re-tender. Logging it as a pure strength is exactly how a concentration risk catches you by surprise.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>Concentration is very much a portfolio issue.</strong> When 28% of revenue rides on one renewing academy, the shape of your portfolio <em>is</em> your risk profile. It belongs in the strategy diagnosis, not parked with sales.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You ranked by contribution per faculty-day, named the capacity-heavy academy as the dilutive hog, and flagged the 28% client as crown-jewel-and-risk. That's the map that makes every later reallocation defensible.`,
                    coach_r2_tilt:          `<strong>That's the reallocation the ceiling rewards.</strong> Moving faculty-days from low-yield-per-day work toward your highest contribution-per-day lines lifts contribution without adding a single day. This is where most of your €0.50M gap gets closed.`,
                    coach_r2_protect:       `<strong>Tilting toward the academies protects revenue and dilutes yield.</strong> Customised work is the lowest contribution per faculty-day you have — pour more scarce days into it and you spend your capacity on your weakest yield. Revenue grows; the contribution gap doesn't close.`,
                    coach_r2_flat:          `<strong>Running last year again won't move the number.</strong> The board didn't ask for the same portfolio at the same yield — a flat mix leaves the €0.50M gap exactly where it was. Reshaping the mix is the lever; you've declined to pull it.`,
                    coach_r2_value:         `<strong>Value pricing chases volume on a thin margin.</strong> Cutting price to fill seats can lift headcount and lower contribution at the same time. The profit-maximising tier is the one where margin × fill is highest — not the cheapest.`,
                    coach_r2_premium:       `<strong>Premium pricing on prestige ignores the demand curve.</strong> Push price past what the curve bears and fill rate falls faster than margin rises. Prestige is not a pricing strategy; the elasticity is.`,
                    coach_r2_cutboth:       `<strong>You axed both flagships on standalone margin.</strong> Clean math — but one of them is the discovery funnel that 40% of your academy clients first experienced you through. Kill the funnel and next year's high-value pipeline thins. A programme's portfolio role can exceed its own margin.`,
                    coach_r2_flagignore:    `<strong>Leaving both under-filled flagships untouched is a decision too.</strong> At 64% and 71% fill they drag contribution and burn faculty-days while you hope. Reprice, redesign, or convert them — hoping is not a portfolio move.`,
                    coach_r2_overbook:      `<strong>You've booked faculty above the 5,600-day ceiling.</strong> On the spreadsheet you hit the number — by running utilisation past 100%. Two senior instructors already said they're cutting days. When they walk, you cancel cohorts mid-delivery, refund participants, and the contribution you booked evaporates. The ceiling is real.`,
                    coach_r3_focus:         `<strong>One credible franchise, properly sized.</strong> Backing a single high-demand sector with ~300–450 faculty-days builds something that reaches critical mass and pays back — exactly the focus a capacity ceiling forces. Relevance and contribution both move.`,
                    coach_r3_sector:        `<strong>One sector chosen — now size it for critical mass.</strong> A credible launch needs roughly 300–450 faculty-days; much less and it never reaches the visibility to pay back. Focus is right; the dosage matters.`,
                    coach_r3_starved:       `<strong>You picked one sector but starved it.</strong> Below critical mass a new franchise stays invisible — too little marketing, too few cohorts, no reputation. Focus without enough capacity to land it is almost as weak as spreading thin.`,
                    coach_r3_scatter:       `<strong>You funded all four sectors at once.</strong> Add it up — that's roughly 1,400 faculty-days you don't have, and every launch is thinned below critical mass. You can build one credible new franchise this year or four nobody remembers. This overruns the ceiling <em>and</em> wins no relevance.`,
                    coach_r3_restructure:   `<strong>That defends the anchor without surrendering margin.</strong> A modular, partly-digital redesign holds volume and protects contribution — a genuine quid pro quo instead of a blink. The 28% client stays, and your gap doesn't widen.`,
                    coach_r3_concede:       `<strong>You gave the bank its 12% with nothing in return.</strong> That's roughly €320k of contribution gone in one signature — two-thirds of your entire €0.50M gap, re-opened. What did you get back: a longer term, more cohorts, an exclusivity clause? Or did you just blink because they're 28% of the book?`,
                    coach_r3_hold:          `<strong>Holding price and daring them to walk is a real gamble.</strong> They're 28% of revenue and they came to the table for a reason. Refuse any movement with no alternative on offer and you risk losing volume — sometimes the whole relationship. Hold is not the same as defend.`,
                    coach_r3_overbook:      `<strong>You funded the new sector by pushing past 100% utilisation.</strong> Growth bought with capacity you don't have isn't growth — it's the over-booking trap with a new label. Fund the franchise from days you freed inside the ceiling, not on top of it.`,
                    coach_r3_anchorcut:     `<strong>You stripped faculty from the anchor academy mid-delivery.</strong> Gutting the crown jewel to fund a new bet is how you turn a renewing 28% client into a lost one — a €1M+ hole that no growth sector closes this year.`,
                    coach_r4_absorb:        `<strong>That's resilience by design.</strong> Because you kept a little slack and a diversified mix, the shock lands on a portfolio built to take it — you rebalance instead of unravelling. The +25% holds even when the board throws the curveball.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>“No shock will hit” is the one plan the board won't accept.</strong> A portfolio run with zero slack and a single concentrated client is precisely the one a shock breaks. Refusing to stress-test it doesn't make it resilient — it makes you unprepared.`,
                    coach_r4_panic:         `<strong>Panic-cutting the new sector and re-discounting the anchor destroys the work.</strong> You undo your growth bet and surrender margin in the same breath — the shock didn't break the plan, the reaction did. A resilient portfolio absorbs; a fragile one improvises.`,
                    coach_r4_revenue:       `<strong>Leading with revenue invites the obvious probe.</strong> “You're bigger — so why is contribution barely up?” The board set a contribution target, not a revenue one. Top-line growth that doesn't convert to margin per faculty-day is not the win they asked for.`,
                    coach_r4_prestige:      `<strong>Prestige is a nice slide, not an answer to the mandate.</strong> The rector asked for €0.50M of contribution within the ceiling. “The academies make us famous” doesn't close the gap — lead with the number and the yield that produced it.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>That owns the trade-off honestly.</strong> Naming what the capacity bought, what you gave up, and why the portfolio now survives a shock is exactly what a board trusts. You hit the number, stayed inside the ceiling, defended the anchor — and you can show why.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>Promising +40% next year to look ambitious is a trap you're setting for yourself.</strong> You've just told the board the ceiling has more in it than it does. Next year's gap starts impossible, and the credibility you spent this year evaporates.`,
                    coach_r4_deflect:       `<strong>Blaming procurement and the market is the unit abdicating its mandate.</strong> Yes, in-company is procurement-pressured and the anchor squeezed — managing exactly that was the job. The board hears an excuse where it wanted a portfolio decision.`,

